Well, good thing I didn't review it in detail.

You lead off with an anecdotal assumption, and then presume to do
complex statistical analysis on it?  You've got to be kidding me.

The average number of potions generated on any given level is certainly
one way to approach this, but it needs to take the following factors 
into account:

-- Potions generated on the floor
-- Potions generated as part of chest loot
-- Potions generated on monsters
-- Potions generated as monster death-drops
-- Potions generated inside a liquor store
-- Potions generated inside a general store
-- Potions from bones files (healers matter here)

Are you starting to see why it's easier to build a large source of data
to do analysis from by simply tracking over a large number of player 
games -- say, from NAO -- and tracking how many potions were _actually_ 
generated?

One big reason here is that #3 and #4 on that list are _entirely_
dependent on player playstyle.  Someone who hangs around doing a lot of
sacfesting on a level will have a lot more monsters generated; as such,
they will see more deathdrops and more potions generated on monsters.
Someone who cleans out a level rapidly and descends will see fewer
potions.

The only way to normalize data such as this and incorporate a wide
variety of player playstyles is to pull data from actual games (ie. my
method), and then come back later and say "This is the average -- if you
spend less time on levels you will see fewer, and if you spend more time
on levels you will see more".

